Aim
To visualize layers, activations, and loss using Keras and build a 2–3 layer DNN for digit classification.
Theory
A Deep Neural Network (DNN) is an artificial neural network containing multiple layers of interconnected neurons. It is used to learn patterns from data and perform classification.

In this practical, we use the MNIST dataset, which contains 60,000 training images and 10,000 testing images of handwritten digits from 0 to 9. Each image has a size of 28 × 28 pixels.

The dataset is loaded using TensorFlow. Pixel values are normalized from 0–255 to 0–1. The Sequential model is used to create the neural network. The Flatten layer converts the image into a one-dimensional array. Dense layers use ReLU and Softmax activation functions for digit classification.

The model is compiled using the Adam optimizer, sparse categorical cross-entropy loss function and accuracy metric. It is trained for 5 epochs with a batch size of 32.
Finally, the model is evaluated using testing data, and a sample handwritten digit is predicted.

Conclusion
Thus, a Deep Neural Network was successfully implemented using TensorFlow and Keras for handwritten digit classification using the MNIST dataset.








#pip install tensorflow matplotlib

# 1. Import libraries
import tensorflow as tf
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np

from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense, Flatten


# 2. Load MNIST dataset
(x_train, y_train), (x_test, y_test) = tf.keras.datasets.mnist.load_data()

print("Training data:", x_train.shape)
print("Testing data:", x_test.shape)


# 3. Display sample digit images
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))

for i in range(10):
    plt.subplot(2, 5, i + 1)
    plt.imshow(x_train[i], cmap="gray")
    plt.title(f"Digit: {y_train[i]}")
    plt.axis("off")

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()


# 4. Normalize pixel values from 0-255 to 0-1
x_train = x_train / 255.0
x_test = x_test / 255.0


# 5. Build a 3-layer neural network
model = Sequential([
    Flatten(input_shape=(28, 28)),
    Dense(128, activation="relu"),
    Dense(64, activation="relu"),
    Dense(10, activation="softmax")
])


# 6. Display network architecture
model.summary()


# 7. Compile the model
model.compile(
    optimizer="adam",
    loss="sparse_categorical_crossentropy",
    metrics=["accuracy"]
)


# 8. Train the model
history = model.fit(
    x_train, y_train,
    epochs=5,
    batch_size=32,
    validation_split=0.1
)


# 9. Evaluate the model
test_loss, test_accuracy = model.evaluate(x_test, y_test)

print("Test Loss:", test_loss)
print("Test Accuracy:", test_accuracy)


# 10. Visualize training and validation loss
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))

plt.plot(history.history["loss"], label="Training Loss")
plt.plot(history.history["val_loss"], label="Validation Loss")

plt.title("Training vs Validation Loss")
plt.xlabel("Epoch")
plt.ylabel("Loss")
plt.legend()
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()


# 12. Visualize the neural network architecture
tf.keras.utils.plot_model(
    model,
    show_shapes=True,
    show_layer_names=True,
    to_file="dnn_architecture.png"
)


# 13. Predict a sample digit
predictions = model.predict(x_test[:1])
predicted_digit = np.argmax(predictions[0])

plt.imshow(x_test[0], cmap="gray")
plt.title(f"Actual: {y_test[0]}, Predicted: {predicted_digit}")
plt.axis("off")
plt.show()



